      <h1 class="style48">About Schemes </h1>
      <p align="justify">Government schemes are structured programs launched by national or state governments to promote the welfare and development of various sections of society. These schemes are designed to address specific issues such as poverty, unemployment, education, healthcare, agriculture, housing, and social security. Each scheme is targeted at a particular group like women, children, farmers, students, senior citizens, or economically weaker sections based on their needs and eligibility criteria.</p><br> <br/>
          <p align="justify">The aim of these schemes is to provide financial assistance, skill development, employment opportunities, subsidies, health benefits, and other forms of support to improve living standards and promote inclusive growth. By simplifying access to essential services and resources, government schemes empower citizens and bridge social and economic gaps. They play a vital role in national development by ensuring that benefits reach the right people at the right time, often through digital platforms and structured application processes.</p>
          <p align="justify">Government schemes are policy driven initiatives launched by the government to meet specific developmental, economic, or social goals. These schemes are tailored to address the needs of diverse sections of society, especially vulnerable and marginalized groups. Whether its providing free education, affordable healthcare, employment opportunities, or financial aid, schemes are designed to ensure that citizens have equitable access to resources and services that can improve their quality of life.</p><br><br />
              <p align="justify">Schemes can be broadly categorized into sectors such as education, health, employment, women empowerment, agriculture, housing, and rural development. For example, educational schemes aim to reduce dropout rates and promote higher education by offering scholarships and fee waivers to underprivileged students. Health related schemes provide insurance coverage and free medical services to low income families. Similarly, employment schemes focus on skill training, job creation, and income support, especially in rural and semi urban areas.</p>
          
          
